<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hughes–Peynado plan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hughes–Peynado agreement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hughes–Peynado plan was the 1922 agreement between the American secretary of state Charles Evans Hughes and the Dominican lawyer Francisco Peynado that set the terms for ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. It provided for a provisional Dominican government, elections and the recognition of the occupation's legal acts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horacio-vasquez">Horacio Vásquez</a> was elected president in 1924, and the last American troops left that year.</p></div>
